  <div class="col" style="margin-top:14px">
    <h3>Try this</h3>
    <ul class="tight">
      <li><em>One load at the middle</em> puts 40 kN at the middle of the beam, and each support carries 20 kN. The cut lies 2 m from A, so the left part carries nothing but the reaction at A. The shear force at the cut is therefore 20 kN, and the bending moment is 20 kN multiplied by 2 m, which is 40 kN·m.</li>
      <li>Drag the cut slowly toward B. As far as the load the shear force stays at 20 kN and the moment grows by 20 kN·m for every metre, until it reaches 80 kN·m under the load. With the cut exactly at the load the shear force tile shows two values, 20 and −20 kN, because the shear force drops by the whole load there. Beyond the load the moment falls again and is back at zero at B.</li>
      <li><em>One load 3 m from A</em> moves the same load toward A, which now carries 25 kN while B carries 15 kN. The cut lies 5 m from A, beyond the load, where the shear force is −15 kN and the moment is 45 kN·m. The largest moment is still found under the load, but it is 75 kN·m and no longer 80 kN·m.</li>
      <li><em>Even load</em> spreads the same 40 kN over the whole beam as 5 kN on every metre. Each support carries 20 kN as in the first preset, but the shear force now falls evenly from 20 kN at A to −20 kN at B, and the moment diagram is a curve with its largest value at the middle, where the shear force passes zero. That value is 40 kN·m, half of what the same weight gave when it stood at the middle.</li>
      <li><em>Fixed, load at the end</em> takes the roller away and lets a fixed support at A hold the beam alone, with 40 kN on the free end. The shear force is 40 kN at every cut. The moment is negative everywhere, which means that the top side is stretched, and the diagram has moved above the line. It is −240 kN·m at the cut and −320 kN·m at the support, and it is zero at the free end.</li>
      <li><em>Fixed, even load</em> has the same 40 kN spread along the fixed beam. The support still carries 40 kN, but the moment at the support is −160 kN·m, half of what it was with the load on the free end, because the middle of the load is only half as far out.</li>
      <li>Press <em>Reset all</em> and then drag the load from 40 to 60 kN. The line under the tiles then reads shear force at the cut +50 %, moment at the cut +50 %, largest moment +50 %. Every section force grows in step with the load. The two diagrams keep their shape, although the numbers on their axes may jump to make room for the larger values.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
